This file is a placeholder, not a secret, and not encrypted. It holds the path open so the wiring
around it can be read and reviewed before there is a real certificate to put here.

There is deliberately no encrypted stand-in beside it. Encrypting one would have meant generating a
GPG key and committing it, and a key in a public repository is not a key.

To replace this with the real thing, once you have made a key and put its fingerprint in .sops.yaml
(see README.md):

    sops --encrypt --input-type binary --output-type binary \
        real-certificate.pfx > secrets/signing.pfx.enc

    git rm secrets/signing.pfx.enc.example
    git add secrets/signing.pfx.enc
    git commit -m "chore(secrets): the release signing certificate"

Then set the two repository secrets CI needs, as README.md describes:

    gpg --export-secret-keys --armor <FINGERPRINT> | base64 -w0 > key.txt
    gh secret set GPG_PRIVATE_KEY < key.txt
    gh secret set GPG_PASSPHRASE
    shred -u key.txt

Until all of that is done the Windows release is signed with the sample certificate in .signing/, which
is a real signature by an authority nobody trusts. That is the intended state for a project without a
code signing certificate: visibly unsigned beats silently unsigned.
